  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist in Kibana. The rule is enabled and running.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-dbafda31 Web logs overview`**\n- ID: `eff-dbafda31-web-logs-overview-a25473229a5f`\n- Five panels, all on the `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view:\n  - **Total requests**: count metric.\n  - **Average response bytes**: average of `bytes`.\n  - **Responses by status code**: pie chart of `response.keyword`, top 10.\n  - **Requests over time**: line chart, date histogram on `timestamp`.\n  - **5xx responses**: ES|QL metric counting `response` values matching `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- Default time range is the last year, since the sample data's timestamps may be older than the last 7 days. Adjust it if you want a different window.\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-dbafda31 5xx spike`**\n- ID: `5fdd6e6e-8783-4f9a-95c9-dc6cf6181777`\n- Type `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n- Query matches `response.keyword` against the regexp `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window. Checks every 1 minute.\n- Status is `ok`. It has no connector attached, so it will record alerts but not notify anyone.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- I enabled the rule. The request implied it should run, and you can disable it with `disable_alert_rule`.\n- I tagged the rule `EFF-dbafda31`.\n\n**Limitations:**\n- I couldn't run aggregations against the data directly. The local Elasticsearch port isn't reachable and Kibana's query proxies are disabled. Exploration was therefore based on the data view's field list, not on the actual values or volumes. I haven't confirmed that the 5xx panel or the rule return data, so open the dashboard to check that the panels render.",
